## 2. Typeface Roboto

- **Work:** Roboto Variable (v51), latin subset
- **Author:** The Roboto Project Authors, commissioned by Google Fonts
- **Source:** https://github.com/googlefonts/roboto-3-classic
- **File origin:** served by fonts.gstatic.com for the Google Fonts CSS API
- **Licence:** SIL Open Font License 1.1
  (https://openfontlicense.org), full text in
  `assets/fonts/LICENSE-roboto.txt`
- **Files:** `assets/fonts/roboto-latin.woff2`
- **Modification statement:** none. The file is redistributed byte-identical as
  served by Google Fonts, so the reserved name Roboto still applies.

Licence history, recorded because it is a trap: Roboto v2, the static family,
was published under Apache 2.0. The variable family used here is OFL 1.1. A
project that copies the licence from an old tutorial will state the wrong one.

## 3. Material Design 3 colour tokens

- **Work:** Material Design 3 design system, colour roles and elevation values
- **Author:** Google LLC
- **Source:** https://m3.material.io/
- **Licence:** Apache License 2.0
  (https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0)
- **Usage:** colour role names and palette values were transcribed into CSS
  custom properties in `styles.css`. No Material code or component library is
  bundled.

## 4. Music

The background music is generated at runtime from note data. There are no audio
files and no recordings: every tune is a synthetic performance of a melody held
as plain data in `assets/music/tracks.js`.

The melodies are traditional, except where noted. Each was transcribed to ABC
notation on a public folk-music archive, and that ABC was converted to note
data with `music21` at authoring time; nothing was copied from a recording,
MIDI file or modern arrangement. The `source` field in `assets/music/tracks.js`
and the table below name that archive page.

**Copyright position.** This project is published in Portugal, where copyright
runs for 70 years from the year after the author's death. Every tune below meets
that test except **Cielito Lindo**, whose author died in 1957, so protection ends
on 31 December 2027. It is included with its author credited; the music can be
switched off with the radio button.

| Tune | Credit | Country | Source |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Soldier's Joy | Traditional | Ireland / USA | https://thesession.org/tunes/1356 |
| Waltzing Matilda | Traditional (words: Banjo Paterson) | Australia | https://thesession.org/tunes/16232 |
| Yankee Doodle | Traditional | USA | https://thesession.org/tunes/10574 |
| Turkey in the Straw | Traditional | USA | https://thesession.org/tunes/2638 |
| The Irish Washerwoman | Traditional | Ireland | https://thesession.org/tunes/92 |
| Pop Goes the Weasel | Traditional | England | https://thesession.org/tunes/10534 |
| Arkansas Traveler | Traditional | USA | https://thesession.org/tunes/5583 |
| Buffalo Gals | Traditional | USA | https://thesession.org/tunes/24492 |
| Drunken Sailor | Traditional | Ireland / UK | https://thesession.org/tunes/15961 |
| Old Joe Clark | Traditional | USA | https://thesession.org/tunes/14474 |
| The Sailor's Hornpipe | Traditional | UK | https://thesession.org/tunes/12708 |
| La Cucaracha | Traditional | Mexico | https://abcnotation.com (musicaviva mirror) |
| She'll Be Coming 'Round the Mountain | Traditional | USA | https://abcnotation.com (Thornton Rose mirror) |
| Alouette | Traditional | Canada (Quebec) | https://abcnotation.com (Digital Tradition mirror) |
| Oh! Susanna | Stephen Foster (1848) | USA | https://abcnotation.com (Nottingham Music Database) |
| Cielito Lindo | Quirino Mendoza y Cortes (1882) | Mexico | https://abcnotation.com (John Chambers) |
| Old MacDonald Had a Farm | Traditional | USA / UK | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_MacDonald_Had_a_Farm |
| Tarantella Napoletana | Traditional | Italy (Naples) | https://abcnotation.com (demo tunes) |

Notes on two tunes:

- **Old MacDonald Had a Farm.** The only ABC transcription found was licensed
  CC BY-NC-SA, which this project cannot use. The melody is public domain, so it
  was written out from the standard tune instead. It is the one melody here not
  derived from a permissively licensed transcription.
- **The Irish Washerwoman** and **The Sailor's Hornpipe** were each transcribed
  under an alternate title in the archives used (`Corporal Casey` and `College
  Hornpipe`). They are the same traditional tunes under their other names.

## 5. Tone.js

- **Work:** Tone.js 14.8.49
- **Author:** Yotam Mann and contributors
- **Source:** https://github.com/Tonejs/Tone.js
- **File origin:** https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/tone/14.8.49/Tone.js
- **Licence:** MIT, full text in `assets/vendor/tone/LICENSE.md`
- **Files:** `assets/vendor/tone/Tone.js`, `assets/vendor/tone/LICENSE.md`,
  `assets/vendor/tone/Tone.js.LICENSE.txt`
- **Modification statement:** none. The file is redistributed byte-identical as
  published by cdnjs.

It is vendored rather than loaded from a CDN so that the app keeps its promise
of making no third-party request and working offline.

## 6. Nothing else

No other third-party content is included. There are no audio files, no
analytics, no icon fonts. Sound effects are generated at runtime by the Web
Audio API inside `app.js`.
